{"title":"2012 AMC 8 Problems/Problem 15","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_2(Chinese_Remainder_Theorem)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2(Chinese Remainder Theorem)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_2(Chinese_Remainder_Theorem-Method_algebric)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2(Chinese Remainder Theorem-Method algebric)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_15&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The smallest number greater than 2 that leaves a remainder of 2 when divided by 3, 4, 5, or 6 lies between what numbers?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/8/4/3840b6b58550281038065e558f60f930627801ef.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A)}\\hspace{.05in}40\\text{ and }50\\qquad\\textbf{(B)}\\hspace{.05in}51\\text{ and }55\\qquad\\textbf{(C)}\\hspace{.05in}56\\text{ and }60\\qquad\\textbf{(D)}\\hspace{.05in}61\\text{ and }65\\qquad\\textbf{(E)}\\hspace{.05in}66\\text{ and }99$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"676\" height=\"17\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution\">Video Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_15&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/rQUwNC0gqdg?t=172\">https://youtu.be/rQUwNC0gqdg?t=172</a>\n</p><p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Vfsb4nwvopU\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Vfsb4nwvopU</a>   ~David\n</p><p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/hOnw5UtBSqI\">https://youtu.be/hOnw5UtBSqI</a> ~savannahsolver\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_15&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>To find the answer to this problem, we need to find the least common multiple of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/1/601a7806cbfad68196c43a4665871f8c3186802e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and add <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> to the result. To calculate the least common multiple, we need to find the prime factorization for each of them. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/b/65b898b621bc5d54725464a2b3d2047a74f460cd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3 = 3^{1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"52\" height=\"17\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/6/3/463f0c2cdc8a7058b8337caf5cf9327c8aa166b9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4 = 2^{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"52\" height=\"17\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/b/3eb9c378e57e3291577d6673dde72a216c726a0f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5 = 5^{1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"52\" height=\"17\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/0/8/4082273411f898973305450a5078fcfe0de81f16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6 = 2^{1}*{3^{1}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"85\" height=\"17\" />. So the least common multiple of the four numbers is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/8/7/4871d5a7def3b49bd88abcbe1d5ad0500b94575b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{2}*{3^{1}*{5^{1}}} = 60$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"128\" height=\"17\" />, and by adding <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, we find that that such number is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/6/3e67376d85a21889a01b76d7c9d8c765b2bac379.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$62$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />. Now we need to find the only given range that contains <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/6/3e67376d85a21889a01b76d7c9d8c765b2bac379.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$62$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />. The only such range is answer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/3/0/b305929a95b53f14a0ad53eba1d8560626e1f28b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(D)}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"31\" height=\"18\" />, and so our final answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/4/2f4ba7a4df1cc49b8c81b11a63d339440f5ae6cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(D)}\\ 61\\text{ and }65}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"121\" height=\"28\" />.\n</p><p>~NXC\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_2.28Chinese_Remainder_Theorem.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2(Chinese_Remainder_Theorem)\">Solution 2(Chinese Remainder Theorem)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_15&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2(Chinese Remainder Theorem)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We have to find the smallest number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/e/2/8e2cf151624a43ccee7efdbc470c09878715f6cd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x \\equiv 2\\mod3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"108\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/e/79e8c406c730bb3387f317ed8dff940d48412077.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x \\equiv 2\\mod4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"108\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/9/b691f77fe718d2d91504ba505ea2e1ed5d888cf1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x \\equiv 2\\mod5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"108\" height=\"14\" />. We are excluding six because it won't make our divisors relatively prime. For the first equation, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/e/2/8e2cf151624a43ccee7efdbc470c09878715f6cd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x \\equiv 2\\mod3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"108\" height=\"14\" /> holds remainder(<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/5/b55ca7a0aa88ab7d58f4fc035317fdac39b17861.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />) of 2. Now we have to find <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/1/5d1e4485dc90c450e8c76826516c1b2ccb8fce16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"21\" height=\"14\" />. Our <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/1/5d1e4485dc90c450e8c76826516c1b2ccb8fce16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"21\" height=\"14\" /> which is all divisors multiplied by each other divided by the divisor we are using. So our <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/1/5d1e4485dc90c450e8c76826516c1b2ccb8fce16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"21\" height=\"14\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/7/4/b74d7747d1074d850164aa472fcbc95f560a5691.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{3 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 5}{3}=20$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"102\" height=\"38\" />. We now need to find the inverse of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> times <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/1/5d1e4485dc90c450e8c76826516c1b2ccb8fce16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"21\" height=\"14\" /> and solve <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />. Doing this we can get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/0/e/70e619df2507162ab9a67fd1916a094680459c2b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$20a \\equiv 1\\mod3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"125\" height=\"14\" />, and the smallest value of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> where this is true is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. Now we multiply every value we have which gets us <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/f/0af91b349209a57b69813c0bfd77b5d5c0277c2c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2 \\cdot 20 \\cdot 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"64\" height=\"14\" />. Then we repeat for mod 4 and mod 5. For mod 4 the numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/5/b55ca7a0aa88ab7d58f4fc035317fdac39b17861.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />,<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/1/5d1e4485dc90c450e8c76826516c1b2ccb8fce16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"21\" height=\"14\" />,<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/1/9/f1965fae079a9ba2c0726c307070c2355dfcb213.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$15$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> respectively. For mod 5 we get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />,<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/f/edf074831eb5bc9e61d6d6e09f525a86e3068f6a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$12$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/5/8455f3b5cb3b4880b8c9d782a5c1f0334db819eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. Now we have to multiply all our values and we get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/e/a/fea5fcc6d66685ee21f722efcd228eb44d38f262.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$80$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> (mod 3), <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/c/bfcfee7bd8a5e15e5f6b7881c138de43651572a9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$90$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> (mod 4), and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/9/4/b940d283ec4a89f128f2d093ce24b514ac8377d6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$192$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"26\" height=\"13\" /> (mod 5). Now we do <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/2/0/6203d0d76d75a6b4b38b98485ad295794188150f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$80+90+192\\mod{5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"216\" height=\"16\" />. This is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/4/5d4bea4be82711c68084db576aea72000046b85e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$362\\mod60$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"100\" height=\"14\" />. This means <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/a/0/7a0bb036ac4b7d677a8bd5e4d365d959e80923f6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x \\equiv 2\\mod60$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"117\" height=\"14\" />, but since it needs to be greater than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, we get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/6/3e67376d85a21889a01b76d7c9d8c765b2bac379.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$62$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />, which divided by six also has a remainder of 2. So <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/6/3e67376d85a21889a01b76d7c9d8c765b2bac379.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$62$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> is in the range of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/4/2f4ba7a4df1cc49b8c81b11a63d339440f5ae6cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(D)}\\ 61\\text{ and }65}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"121\" height=\"28\" />.\n</p><p>If CRT didn't make sense, this video is very good:\n</p><p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MdePzlQtnCc\">Chinese Remainder Theorem | Sun Tzu's Theorem</a>\n</p><p>Additionally, this page is great:\n</p><p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php/Chinese_Remainder_Theorem\">CRT</a>\n</p><p>~<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php/User:Am24\">AM24</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_2.28Chinese_Remainder_Theorem-Method_algebric.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2(Chinese_Remainder_Theorem-Method_algebric)\">Solution 2(Chinese Remainder Theorem-Method algebric)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_15&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2(Chinese Remainder Theorem-Method algebric)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> the number of problem. \n</p><p>By Chinese Remainder Theorem, we know that\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/3/8d3a6b71d12b99fcc734672c79fd44c05dc04001.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\begin{cases}  x \\equiv 2 \\pmod 3 \\\\ x \\equiv 2 \\pmod 4 \\\\ x \\equiv 2 \\pmod 5  \\end{cases}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -34px\" width=\"152\" height=\"77\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/4/5/c45a4dcdd50498d8f427e0a9f772d9c19b8a11b2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x \\equiv 2 \\pmod 6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"118\" height=\"20\" /> is exclude because it won't make our divisors relatively prime. \nThe solution in this problem is:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/f/9/cf9c34a9bb003c2551a00dc77bbdfc23065affc9.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[x \\equiv  \\sum^{n=3}_{k=1} a_kb_kc_k \\pmod {4 \\times 3 \\times 5} \\implies x\\equiv  \\sum^{n=3}_{k=1} a_kb_kc_k \\pmod {60}\\]\" width=\"509\" height=\"51\" />\n</p><p>where:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/f/c3fa9570460e51d259d39ec66ddef57518fd7458.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_k: \\text {The product of all moduli except the k-th modulus.}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"411\" height=\"17\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/7/8/47844b0db2c85f1ef8a097ec4d1ecdbecca79af3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b_k: \\text{Inverse modular}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"150\" height=\"17\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/c/f/2cfcad50253b6bd07e208c044187fda44811f9ce.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c_k: \\text{remainder of x by k-th modulus}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"265\" height=\"17\" />\n</p><p>Since the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/f/dcfd919c61951723b88afd5cce1382776263a8a4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c_1=c_2=c_3=2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"130\" height=\"17\" />:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/7/e/07e5219b21e28f7a214fd2f3c61070b8ce34f19e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[a_1=\\frac{3\\times 4 \\times 5}{3} = 20 \\implies 20b_1 \\equiv 1 \\pmod 3 \\implies 2b_1 \\equiv 1 \\pmod 3 \\therefore b_1=2\\]\" width=\"617\" height=\"38\" />\n</p><p><br />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/8/c/e8c820444b851e838880f6d620f83c5de4c0bd87.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[a_2=\\frac{3\\times 4 \\times 5}{4} = 15 \\implies 15b_2 \\equiv 1 \\pmod 4 \\implies 3b_2 \\equiv 1 \\pmod 4 \\therefore b_2=3\\]\" width=\"617\" height=\"38\" />\n</p><p><br />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/f/a/8faa34a7f463c1d72c44fbdecb863b059fd93cf0.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[a_3=\\frac{3\\times 4 \\times 5}{5} = 12 \\implies 12b_2 \\equiv 1 \\pmod 5 \\implies 2b_2 \\equiv 1 \\pmod 5 \\therefore b_3=3\\]\" width=\"617\" height=\"38\" />\n</p><p>therefore\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/5/9/1593700ecd4376782a302cb7ca491026377067ac.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[x\\equiv  \\sum^{n=3}_{k=1} a_kb_kc_k \\pmod {60} \\Rightarrow x\\equiv 20 \\times 2 \\times 2 + 15 \\times 2 \\times 3 + 12 \\times 2 \\times 3\\pmod {60} \\Rightarrow x\\equiv  242 \\pmod {60} \\Rightarrow x\\equiv  2 \\pmod {60}\\]\" width=\"924\" height=\"51\" />\n</p><p>Thus, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/e/0/ee02d5e257d3d795ecb2039250e057603b32e516.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=60m+2&gt;2 \\implies m&gt;0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"234\" height=\"15\" /> and for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/2/6/6262426a50b108855e18767dc3bc5467d61ba0aa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m=1 \\implies x=62$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"153\" height=\"14\" />. \n</p><p>Correct answer: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/b/55bdade53f471aab49f63d1a734979cc0c636640.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{D}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"29\" height=\"25\" />  \n</p><p><br />\n</p><p><br />\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_15&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_8\" title=\"2012 AMC 8\">2012 AMC 8</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_8_Problems\" title=\"2012 AMC 8 Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_8_Answer_Key\" title=\"2012 AMC 8 Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/resources.php?c=182&amp;cid=42&amp;year=2012\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_14\" title=\"2012 AMC 8 Problems/Problem 14\">Problem 14</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_16\" title=\"2012 AMC 8 Problems/Problem 16\">Problem 16</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2012 AMC 8 Problems/Problem 1\